WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.370
Hello and welcome!

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:11.340
In the last video about Transformers, we found&nbsp;
out how the Transformer architecture works.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.200
In this video, you will find out&nbsp;
how the transformer architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.650
can process images and text simultaneously!

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:24.660
But let’s not spoil everything&nbsp;
already and start with the beginning:

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:30.420
The Transformer architecture has proven&nbsp;
to be very impactful in Natural Language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.890
Processing. But what can be even&nbsp;
cooler than looking at only text?

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:39.060
Well… looking at text and images at the same time!

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.100
And because we speak about&nbsp;
two different input types&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.550
being processed by a machine learning algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.630
we are here speaking
about multimodal learning.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:56.760
Ok, but what does this have&nbsp;
to do with Transformers? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.610
they can be applied to multimodal problems&nbsp;
too, like for integrating vision and language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.790
in an unified model!
How cool is that?

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:09.030
This kind of model has been proposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:13.050
almost simultaneously by quite a&nbsp;
number of different researchers.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:19.410
All these papers proposed almost&nbsp;
concurrently the same base model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:24.000
of course with some slight variations,&nbsp;
but with the same basic idea:

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.780
They process language and images simultaneously&nbsp;
with a Transformer architecture, and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:37.410
often the Transformer architecture chosen is
BERT, a bi-directional version of the Transformer.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:44.430
And to it, they add the capability of processing&nbsp;
visual input at the same time with language input.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.570
We will not discuss all of the papers here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:54.270
you can find a comprehensive list of most&nbsp;
of them linked below, in the description.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.410
Discussing all these papers&nbsp;
would not only take a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:04.290
but because of the high topic overlap,&nbsp;
it would be also quite redundant.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:09.570
Instead, Ms. Coffee Bean chose her&nbsp;
favorite paper by the Facebook AI team.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:15.550
She chose this one over all others,&nbsp;
because they provided the code base,&nbsp;

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:20.380
which is not only available for&nbsp;
everyone, but also clean and maintained.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:26.110
In the following, we will discuss&nbsp;
first how image and language are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:30.700
integrated at once in the BERT&nbsp;
Transformer model and second about&nbsp;

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:36.790
the training procedure, which I would&nbsp;
like to call “the pre-train-ception!"

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.340
Ok, so let’s dive into it!

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:47.110
From now on, we will call this the “Transformer&nbsp;
module”, which is formed like we have explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:51.899
in the last video, so it is formed of the&nbsp;
multi-head self-attention layer, the feed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.899 --> 00:02:57.700
forward layers and the residual connections&nbsp;
acting like information highways between them.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.270
So this everything now is the transformer module.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:08.110
The trick here is to train the model on&nbsp;
text-image pairs, like images and their captions.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:15.580
The text part is easy to explain, because it is&nbsp;
exactly like what the usual BERT architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:22.030
does: we feed the word or tokens in&nbsp;
vector representation together after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:27.190
we added the positional embeddings&nbsp;
or identifiers to these vectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:33.430
so our model is informed about the word&nbsp;
order by these positional embeddings.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:39.070
And we feed this to our usual&nbsp;
Transformer Module to process the text.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:47.050
Now let’s put the text part to the side a little&nbsp;
bit and see what we can do on the image side.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:54.280
We also have to somehow transform our&nbsp;
image into a vector sequence format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.280 --> 00:04:00.700
because the Transformer is specialized&nbsp;
for exactly this, sequences. But how?

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:06.880
Well, the idea is: If we combine&nbsp;
vision and language anyway, why not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:13.720
have an architecture that specializes on finding&nbsp;
correspondences between words and image regions?

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.390
Ah! Regions, there we said it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:22.300
let’s extract such image regions and&nbsp;
order them in an arbitrary sequence.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.470
We can represent each region by a vector by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:32.600
employing a CNN-based neural network&nbsp;
that we borrow from computer vision.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.970
Good! We got this far.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:42.170
Now the idea is to really compute these&nbsp;
correspondences between image regions and the text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:50.510
by adding the layers that do this computation with&nbsp;
co-attention. Now what does co-attention mean?

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:56.000
Until now, we had attention, we had&nbsp;
self-attention, now we have co-attention!?

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:03.050
Well, while the usual Transformer modules&nbsp;
in green look only at their own branch for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:09.320
computing attention through self-attention,&nbsp;
the co-attention modules in blue are looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:16.940
also at the other branch to compute importance&nbsp;
scores between images and text and vice-versa.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:22.970
So with co-attention, the image&nbsp;
computes attention based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:29.780
text and vice-versa. For refinement we&nbsp;
add then another layer of Transformers&nbsp;

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:35.810
and another Co-attention layer and another&nbsp;
Transformer module and another… and you get it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:40.790
the deeper the better! We're&nbsp;
doing deep learning here ;)

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:46.160
Now the question is, how do we end&nbsp;
this? How do we get to our output?&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.830
And what is our output anyway?

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:55.250
Well, it depends what we train the network to&nbsp;
do. It can be just a Multi-Layer Perceptron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.250 --> 00:06:00.920
followed by a softmax for getting out a score for&nbsp;
example for the similarity between image and text.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:06.710
This is really dependent on the task and&nbsp;
the whole network can have many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:10.520
output heads, one for each task it is trained on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:15.410
and sometimes, it is trained also&nbsp;
in parallel on many different tasks.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:20.630
So naturally, we have to discuss&nbsp;
what we train this monster to do.

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:28.460
And this Pre-train-ception, how I like to call&nbsp;
it, is a very interesting part! Because like in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:34.700
every inception, we have multiple stages and&nbsp;
here we are ready to explain what Stage 1 is:

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:42.320
We do pre-training at this first stage on the&nbsp;
text side and on the image side, but separately:

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.170
For the text, we have BERT trained&nbsp;
on huge amounts of English text. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:53.330
we take that BERT weights and use them&nbsp;
here, to initialize our BERT modules.

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:59.420
Then, the image side has do be also pre-trained&nbsp;
to produce useful features from the images.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:04.370
Here in the ViLBERT paper,&nbsp;
they have used a Faster-RCNN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:08.180
architecture useful in object&nbsp;
detection for computer vision.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:14.750
For the second stage of pre-training, the&nbsp;
ViLBERT paper uses the Conceptual Captions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:21.320
dataset. They use this one and no other,&nbsp;
because it is huge and contains images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:26.450
and their captions. And on this Conceptual&nbsp;
Captions dataset, they train the network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:31.730
that was already trained on English text&nbsp;
further with the following objectives:

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:36.950
One task is to predict labels for&nbsp;
what the image regions might be.

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:41.180
Another training task is&nbsp;
also followed at this stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.590
which is masked word prediction,&nbsp;
like in the classical Transformer.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:51.230
So here the model would ideally predict&nbsp;
"mountain", because it knows from the image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:56.170
through co-attention layers what&nbsp;
should fall into that place best.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:08:00.130
And we are already at the training stage number 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:06.970
where the same model that at stage 1 learned&nbsp;
to process images and text separately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:13.210
at stage 2 combines them and "understands"&nbsp;
the relations between text and image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:19.780
and is now ready to be trained to solve a task&nbsp;
involving both textual and visual modalities!&nbsp;

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:27.250
Such tasks are for example Visual Question&nbsp;
Answering, where the model is given an image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:33.580
and a question and it has to provide the right&nbsp;
answer to the question, based on the image.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.420
VilBERT not only beat the&nbsp;
state-of-the-art at the time of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:44.980
publication on this task, but by a large margin!
 
Visual Commonsense Reasoning is the better brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:53.200
of VQA, where the system has to provide the&nbsp;
answer to the question, but also to give the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.200 --> 00:09:00.400
reason for the answer, and ideally this reason&nbsp;
is you know… reasoning about the problem and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:06.040
not giving a tautologic explanation like: "This&nbsp;
picture is a mountain because it is a mountain",

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:12.130
but rather an explanation that this&nbsp;
image depicts a mountain because it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:15.880
the mountain is taller than&nbsp;
the horizon, for example.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:21.070
Here ViLBERT also achieves&nbsp;
state-of-the-art by a large margin.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:25.990
Image Retrieval is the task where&nbsp;
given a caption of an image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:31.600
the model has to select the right image&nbsp;
that is described by the caption from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:38.380
a pool of many other images. And surprise,&nbsp;
surprise! State-of-the-art again for ViLBERT.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:44.530
Phrase Grounding is a more fine-grained&nbsp;
task, but also perfect for VilBERT that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:50.860
thanks to the co-attention layers learns&nbsp;
relationships between image regions and words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:56.110
because here in this task, given a&nbsp;
phrase in the caption, like "mountain",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.110 --> 00:10:00.580
the system has to identify the&nbsp;
region that this phrase refers to.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:07.900
And yes, again state-of-the-art for ViLBERT. Yay!
 
So now that you have seen pre-training in so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:15.070
stages, first on text, on images, then on&nbsp;
text and images, and you have seen how this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:21.370
has helped VilBERT to perform so well on&nbsp;
many downstream tasks, we can safely say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:28.870
that this whole pretrain-ception, or you know,&nbsp;
different stages or strategies to pretrain the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:35.650
model before being applied and trained further&nbsp;
on a downstream task, is really, really helpful.

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:36.100
[clap noises]

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:40.810
And because these pretraining&nbsp;
strategies are so cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:43.960
we will spend the whole&nbsp;
next video only about this!

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.870
We will discuss different pre-training strategies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:50.830
because ViLBERT follows only two&nbsp;
of the pre-training strategies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:55.420
but there are others, proposed in other&nbsp;
papers about multimodal transformers.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:11:00.580
So we will see these strategies in the&nbsp;
next video, and not only strategies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:04.990
for multimodal Transformers, but&nbsp;
also for the pure language BERT!

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:15.730
See you then, bye!

